        // Actually, looking at Read(accessor):
        // ```csharp
        // try { return Read(ResolvePath(accessor)); }
        // catch (InvalidOperationException) { return new ...; }
        // ```
        // It only catches InvalidOperationException. If ResolvePath succeeds but
        // Read(string) throws (e.g., IOException), that propagates uncaught!
        //
        // So Path D can happen if Read(string) throws an IOException (e.g.,
        // file locked, permission denied). Then present stays empty but the
        // guard's separate Read call may succeed.
        //
        // This is getting very theoretical. For the test, let me verify that
        // the WARNING code branch compiles and is reachable. I'll simulate it
        // by writing a test that checks the log message when the file exists
        // AND has keys but detection somehow missed them. The simplest way:
        // use a custom IEnvironmentAccessor that causes Read to fail.
